WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.010
Browsers interpret content according to context.

2
00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:09.240
HTML body text, HTML attributes, URLs, JavaScript strings, JSON data, and
CSS each have different rules.

3
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:22.470
The same string can be harmless in one context and unsafe in another
because the browser is not simply displaying characters; it is parsing a
document with rules for markup, navigation, scripting, styling, and data
exchange.

4
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.530
Secure rendering begins by knowing which context receives the data.

5
00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:39.620
Untrusted data can come from many places: user profiles, comments, support
tickets, uploaded files, search terms, third-party APIs, logs, markdown,
rich text editors, administrative fields, and system integrations.

6
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.420
Internal does not automatically mean trusted.

7
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:53.690
A support note entered by an employee, a value imported from a partner, or
a log message shown in an admin portal can still cross into a browser
context and affect users if it is rendered unsafely.

8
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:59.220
Server-side rendering and client-side rendering move the trust boundary in
different ways.

9
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.140
A Django or Jinja template may combine data with HTML on the server.

10
00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.690
A FastAPI or Django API may return JSON that a frontend uses to update the
page.

11
00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.090
A hybrid app may do both.

12
00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:17.980
In every case, data crosses from Python backend to browser frontend, and
the team needs to understand how the final browser-visible output is
created.

13
00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:24.630
Developers should track where data came from, where it is stored, how it
is transformed, and where it is rendered.

14
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:34.220
A value that is validated for length during input may later be used in an
attribute, a URL, a script-adjacent context, a rich-text area, or a
client-side component.

15
00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:41.640
XSS prevention becomes much easier when trust boundaries are visible and
each rendering path has an intentional safety rule.
